---
// /ai-website-seo-checklist: a checklist for people whose site came out of an AI assistant.
// Positioning term (tests/positioning.spec.ts): "SEO checklist for AI-built websites" in the title,
// the description and the H1, and "vibe coded website SEO issues" in the body (the H2 below).
// Plan, proof, route options and review: the private hub's vibe-coded-seo/ folder (2026-10-22).
// Honesty limits for this page: the two Search Engine Journal reports are reported speech about one
// reviewed site and one podcast episode, not rules from Google; the page says "reported", never
// "Google says". Only the three skips in the May 2026 report (canonicals, sitemaps, robots.txt) say
// an assistant leaves something out; the other rows are things worth checking, with no claim about
// how often an assistant gets them wrong. No ranking is promised. Lovable and Bolt are named only in
// one passage, neutrally: who each says it is for, and one dated trade report each; nothing is said about what
// either builds, and no site built with either was tested. The checklist rows and two build-time
// guards (inventory and provenance, not assertion text) are in src/data/ai-website-seo-checklist.ts.
// The breaks behind the rows: docs/ai-website-seo-checklist-breaks.md.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
import { CHECKS, CHECK_COUNT_WORD, NOT_COVERED, QUESTIONS, RED_RUNS, checkedSuites } from '../data/ai-website-seo-checklist';

const REPORT_2025 = 'https://www.searchenginejournal.com/googles-john-mueller-flags-seo-issues-in-vibe-coded-website/558393/';
const REPORT_2026 = 'https://www.searchenginejournal.com/googles-mueller-vibe-coding-wont-handle-your-seo-for-you/574237/';
const LOVABLE_DOCS = 'https://docs.lovable.dev/';
const LOVABLE_TECHCRUNCH = 'https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/25/swedens-lovable-an-app-building-ai-platform-rakes-in-16m-after-spectacular-growth';
const BOLT_HOME = 'https://bolt.new/';
const BOLT_NEWSTACK = 'https://thenewstack.io/how-developers-are-using-bolt-a-fast-growing-ai-coding-tool/';
---
<Base
  title="SEO checklist for AI-built websites"
  description={`SEO checklist for AI-built websites: ${CHECK_COUNT_WORD} checks, the Croftweaver test behind each, what happened when we broke it, and what a pass does not prove.`}
  image="/images/og/ai-website-seo-checklist.jpg"
>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <h1>SEO checklist for AI-built websites</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      An AI assistant can build a website in a matter of days, and nothing makes anyone check what
      search engines make of it. Whether a site was vibe coded in an app builder or built in a terminal
      with a coding assistant, the same question follows: what should be checked? This page lists
      {CHECK_COUNT_WORD} checks, says which of them a Croftweaver test catches, and says what a passing
      test does not prove. Croftweaver sites are built with an AI assistant too; one difference is
      the tests.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href="#checklist">See the checklist</a>
      <a class="btn" href="#reported">What was reported</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="reported">
    <h2>Vibe coded website SEO issues</h2>
    <p>
      Two reports from Search Engine Journal tell what people on Google’s Search Relations team
      said about vibe coded sites and other sites built with an AI assistant. They point in
      opposite directions, too little and too much, and meet at the same place: someone has to
      check.
    </p>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Too much.</strong> In <time datetime="2025-10-15">October 2025</time> the
        <a href={REPORT_2025}>first report</a> told how John Mueller, a Google Search Advocate,
        reviewed on Reddit a website a developer had vibe coded in two days. He named things with no
        value for search on that site: keywords, author and robots meta tags, hreflang tags on a
        one-language site, hidden headings, a sitemap for a single page, and structured data the
        report calls ineffective. He also named key content held in a JavaScript llms.txt file, which he
        said Google does not use. Fixing these, he added, does not bring traffic by itself, but makes
        it easier for search engines to understand a site.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Too little.</strong> In <time datetime="2026-05-07">May 2026</time> the
        <a href={REPORT_2026}>second report</a>, on an episode of Google’s podcast Search Off The
        Record, gave the takeaway of Mueller and Martin Splitt: canonicals, sitemaps and robots.txt
        do not set themselves up because an AI tool built the site. Mueller said he got better results
        by telling the assistant what he wanted from the start, the domain name, the canonical setup,
        the sitemap files and robots.txt, by knowing which site generator he wanted, and by adding
        checks before publishing.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="aside">
      Both are reports of what two people said about one site and one episode. The second adds that
      the episode gave no formal guidance, and that the two were sharing what they had tried. Neither
      is a rule from Google.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some people use AI app builders such as Lovable and Bolt for the same job. This
      page says nothing about what either one builds, and we did not test a site built with either.
      Here is who each says it is for, and what trade press reported:
    </p>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Lovable</strong> describes itself in <a href={LOVABLE_DOCS}>its documentation</a> as
        a full-stack AI development platform for individuals and teams. It names, among others, product managers
        and designers, marketing, sales and operations teams, developers, agencies, founders and
        independent makers. <a href={LOVABLE_TECHCRUNCH}>TechCrunch</a> reported in
        <time datetime="2025-02">February 2025</time> that its founder aims it at the people who
        do not know how to code.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bolt</strong> describes itself on <a href={BOLT_HOME}>its home page</a> as a way to
        create apps and websites by chatting with AI, for product builders. It speaks to business
        owners who want an app without a developer, to teams that prototype, to people who need
        campaign pages, and to students. <a href={BOLT_NEWSTACK}>The New Stack</a> reported in
        <time datetime="2025-02">February 2025</time> that Bolt’s CEO said over half of its users are
        not professional developers, and put the non-technical share at 60 to 70 percent.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="aside">
      These are the companies’ own words and reports of what their founders said, read on
      <time datetime="2026-10-22">22 October 2026</time>. They may have changed since.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="checklist">
    <h2>The checklist</h2>
    <p>
      Each row is one thing to check, the Croftweaver suite that covers it, what happened when we broke
      it, and what a pass does not prove. On <time datetime={RED_RUNS.iso}>{RED_RUNS.label}</time> we
      broke a copy of the Croftweaver starter, version {RED_RUNS.toolkit}, on Node {RED_RUNS.node} (the starter asks for Node {RED_RUNS.nodeStarterAsks}), one thing at a time,
      ran the suite that covers it, and wrote down what failed. You can make the same edits on a copy of your own and
      compare. The suites are described on <a href="/checks">the checks page</a>.
      The checklist works by hand on any site; the tests come with a site built from the Croftweaver
      starter, which needs a coding assistant and a terminal.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="The checks, the suite behind each, and what a pass does not prove" tabindex="0">
      <table role="table">
        <thead role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Check</th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Suite</th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">When we broke it</th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">A pass does not prove</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody role="rowgroup">
          {CHECKS.map((c) => (
            <tr role="row">
              <th scope="row" role="rowheader">{c.check}</th>
              <td role="cell" data-label="Suite">
                {checkedSuites(c).map((s, i) => (
                  <>{i > 0 && ' and '}<a href={`/checks#${s.group}`}>{s.name}</a></>
                ))}
              </td>
              <td role="cell" data-label="When we broke it">{c.broke}</td>
              <td role="cell" data-label="A pass does not prove">{c.limit}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3>What no test here covers</h3>
    <ul class="steps">{NOT_COVERED.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="tell">
    <h2>What you do by hand, and what the starter does</h2>
    <p>
      The checklist works on any site. How much of it you do yourself depends on where the site came from.
    </p>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>A site from another tool</h3>
      <p>You ask for each part, and you check.</p>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li>
          Say what the site needs before the assistant starts: the domain, the canonical address, the
          sitemap and robots.txt, and which site generator to use. The second report says John Mueller of
          Google got better results this way.
        </li>
        <li>Ask for a title and a description on every page, within the limits in the table above, and a share image for each page.</li>
        <li>Ask for a test for each of these that fails when it is wrong, and run the tests every time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="say">
        <span>Say to the assistant:</span>
        <q>Set up the canonical address, the sitemap and robots.txt for example.com from the start.
        Use Astro as the site generator. Write a title and a description for every page within the
        length limits, and make a share image for each page. Add a test for each of them that fails
        when it is wrong.</q>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>A site from the Croftweaver starter</h3>
      <p>The parts come with the site, and so do the tests.</p>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li>The canonical address, the sitemap, robots.txt and structured data are part of the starter.</li>
        <li>Your assistant writes each page’s title and description to the length limits, and one command makes a share image for every page.</li>
        <li>The tests for all of it come with the site, so a wrong canonical address or an overlong title turns the suite red.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p>
      A test gives you something to run every time. It is not a lock. The hook that runs the tests can
      be skipped with one flag, and nothing on the server stops a build that failed from being
      published, as <a href="/checks">the checks page</a> says.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="extras">
    <h2>What Croftweaver ships that the first report questions</h2>
    <p>
      The first report’s list of extras includes things Croftweaver puts on its sites. Here is what each
      one is, and what it is not.
    </p>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li><strong>An llms.txt file.</strong> A short list of a site’s pages for AI assistants. A test keeps it in step with the pages. It does not guarantee that an assistant reads it, and Croftweaver does not say Google uses it.</li>
      <li><strong>Structured data.</strong> Every page carries it, and a test checks that it parses. It does not check that any search engine uses it.</li>
      <li><strong>A robots meta tag.</strong> Every page carries one. The first report names robots meta tags among those with no SEO benefit on the site it covers.</li>
      <li><strong>A sitemap.</strong> The report advised skipping one for a single page. A test keeps this one in step with the site’s pages.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="existing">
    <h2>If your site already exists</h2>
    <p>
      The checklist works by hand on your site. Croftweaver builds a new site and has no import of an
      existing one. Part of it works on a site that is already live, without a rebuild: the SEO audit,
      Search Console insights, the positioning check, the facts check and the weekly AI check. The
      ready-made test suite and the two link audits need the Croftweaver starter.
      <a href="/established-sites">Croftweaver for established sites</a> says what each one does.
      If your site runs on WordPress, <a href="/from-wordpress">the page on moving from WordPress</a>
      covers that case.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap faq" id="questions">
    <h2>Questions and answers</h2>
    {QUESTIONS.map((item) => (
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>{item.q}</h3>
        <div class="faq-answer" set:html={item.a} />
      </div>
    ))}
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/start">Quickstart</a>
      <a class="btn" href={SITE.repo}>Get Croftweaver on GitHub</a>
    </div>
    <p class="aside">
      We read the Search Engine Journal reports on <time datetime="2026-10-22">22 October 2026</time>,
      not the podcast episode, and ran the breaks on that day.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section.wrap { padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem; max-width: var(--maxw); margin: 0 auto; }
  section.hero { padding-top: 3rem; }
  .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); line-height: 1.1; margin: 0 0 1rem; hyphens: manual; }
  h2 { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  section p, .steps { max-width: 46rem; line-height: 1.7; hyphens: manual; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .aside { color: var(--muted); }
  .steps { padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .card p { margin: 0; }
  .say { color: var(--muted); }
  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { width: 100%; max-width: 64rem; border-collapse: collapse; }
  th, td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: 0.55rem 0.8rem 0.55rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line, currentColor); line-height: 1.55; }
  tbody th { font-weight: 600; }
  /* Below 40rem four columns of prose would share 320px or less, so each row stacks: the check as a title,
     then each cell under its own label. The header row stays in the page for screen readers. */
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    table, tbody, tr, th, td { display: block; }
    thead { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap; }
    tbody tr { padding: 1rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line, currentColor); }
    tbody th { padding: 0 0 0.35rem; border: 0; font-size: 1.05rem; }
    td { padding: 0.35rem 0; border: 0; }
    td::before { content: attr(data-label); display: block; font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--muted); }
  }
  .faq-item { padding: 1.1rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .faq-item h3 { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .faq-answer :global(p) { margin: 0 0 0.7rem; max-width: 46rem; line-height: 1.7; hyphens: manual; }
</style>
